Below is a minimal **JUnit 5** example that shows exactly what you asked for –  
*call the helper routine supplied via the *Functions* API*, pass it your real `CSM` instance (`csmInstance`) together with the *fully‑qualified* name of the class whose “invoker” you expect (`MyExpectedClass`).  

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import com.yourcompany.csm.CSM;
import my.package.MyExpectedClass;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE:
//   • The Functions API expects *String representations* of the objects.
//     For ordinary Java objects this means just passing their variable names
//     (the runtime will resolve them). If you prefer to be explicit you
//     could also send e.g. "\"my/package/MyExpectedClass\"".
//
//   • The helper returns a Boolean indicating whether all three conditions
//     hold:
//
//        1) An injected invoker exists on the current thread’s stack.
//        2) That invoker comes from the same module as the expected class.
//        3) It sits above the expected class on the stack frame list.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
class CallerSensitiveMethodTest {

    @org.junit.jupiter.api.Test
    void verifyCallerSensitiveInjectionWorks() throws Exception {

        /* -------------------------------------------------------------
           Arrange:
             - Create / obtain the CSM instance under test.
             - Ensure the caller‑sensitive method has already been invoked so
               that its injection logic ran.
         ------------------------------------------------------------- */
        CSM csmInstance = new CSM();          // <-- replace with real ctor/loader
        // Trigger whatever action causes the invocation to happen
        csmInstance.doSomethingThatIsCallerSensitive();

        /* -------------------------------------------------------------
           Act & Assert:
             Call the helper through the Functions interface and make sure
             it reports success.
         ------------------------------------------------------------- */

        Object result =
                functions.MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker(
                        Map.of("csm", "csmInstance",
                               "expected", "my.package.MyExpectedClass"));

        // The helper returns a primitive boolean wrapped in java.lang.Boolean
        assertNotNull(result);
        assertTrue((Boolean) result,
                   () -> String.format(
                           "Invocation of %s did not inject the correct "
                         + "caller‑sensitive invoker.",
                           MyExpectedClass.class.getName()));
    }
}
```

### What the helper does internally

| Step | Description |
|------|--------------|
| **Stack walk** | Uses `Thread.currentThread().getStackTrace()` to capture the frames at the point where the caller‑sensitive method was executed. |
| **Locate invoker** | Looks for the first frame whose declaring class matches the *module* of `expected`. |
| **Order check** | Confirms that this invoker frame precedes the frame belonging to `expected`. |
| **Return value** | Returns `true` only when all checks succeed; otherwise `false`. |

> **Tip:** If you’re running inside a modular environment (Java 9+), ensure both classes belong to the same named module; otherwise the comparison may fail even though the bytecode looks identical.

---

#### Quick sanity checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✔️ | You have imported the generated `functions.*` package. |
| ✔️ | Your test triggers the caller‑sensitive path before calling the helper. |
| ✔️ | The strings passed match the exact identifiers used by the helper (`"csmInstance"` and `"my.package.MyExpectedClass"`). |
| ✔️ | Assertions report failures clearly enough to pinpoint which condition failed. |

With this setup you’ll immediately know whether the caller‑sensitive mechanism inserted the right invoker class into the stack for your `CSM` instance. Happy testing!

